#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1080.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134684} :activity/id #uuid "52c145b5-305d-5e94-a854-8ca571ca1a03" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050578 :topic/id #uuid "5558fd2c-f6e0-5362-bf8b-7d3500df79a0" :topic/math-academy-id 2127} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134684 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135054 :tutorial/content "The **Gram-Schmidt process** is an algorithm that we can use to construct an *orthogonal* basis if we are given a non-orthogonal basis to start with.\n\nFor example, consider the basis $\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 \\}$ for the subspace $V=\\textrm{Span} \\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 \\},$ where\n![](images/c2/c2543117bc661286a5965534dfeeaaf6a066281ee58e2f39ea35143971d6837f.svg)\n\nWe can use the above basis to construct an *orthogonal* basis for the given $2$-dimensional subspace. To do this, we use the Gram-Schmidt process, which involves $2$ steps:\n\n- **Step 1:** Set ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_1}=\\mathbf{a}_1.$\n\n- **Step 2:** Find a vector $\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2$ in $V$ that's orthogonal to $\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_1.$ This can be achieved by computing\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}\\,-\\,{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}\\,{a}_{2} \\\\  & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/67/678cc056e3c9d3fbf17423ac591b3e85d84ee8060c0637840fe1c4c9e87aa878.png)\n\nNotice that $\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2$ is written as a linear combination of $\\mathbf{a}_1(={\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_1})$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2.$ So this means that ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2} \\in V,$ and also ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2} \\perp {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_1}.$\nMoreover, \n\n$$\n\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2\\} = V = \\textrm{Span}\\{ {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_1}, {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2} \\}.\n$$\n\nAfter applying the Gram-Schmidt process, we get the set $\\mathcal{B}=\\{{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_1}, {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2}\\},$ which is an orthogonal basis for $V.$\n\nSo, in our example, we start by setting ![](images/72/7221215504f7dc7a831501ee23c0563434dd414ac05c1ef7e5761c2719e7d24d.svg) Then, we find the vector $\\mathbf{v}_2$ that's orthogonal to $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{v}_1\\}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{4\\cdot 1+6\\cdot (-1)}{{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]-(-1)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ![](images/59/5944c42ffd5e609dcec914718180be9652a817de6b8cf84439d57cb106f03353.svg) is an orthogonal basis for $V.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "3b833ab7-f6d4-5c93-8709-37ecf29bf023" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3631 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "e23dc2ed-aace-586d-8f71-e7621bc6d386" :step/math-academy-id 20257 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134685}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134685 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133793 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133602 :question/id #uuid "c430a11a-8595-568f-9768-aad8a8de415f" :question/math-academy-id "e-5581"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "16e43d27-ffaf-58ea-bb6e-664b70770003" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048893} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049811}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134309} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134336} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326898} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351261} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354523} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354524} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414751} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414753} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481350} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481360} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481363}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Orthogonal Basis For the Span of Two Vectors"} :step/id #uuid "7dca9c20-2451-5bcc-8034-904527090719" :step/math-academy-id 20258 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134770}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134686 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133794 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133603 :question/id #uuid "47fadc87-f265-5f1c-827a-28e5be9e8121" :question/math-academy-id "e-5582"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b15719b6-0ce4-5fb8-80be-abcaf9344a10" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050656}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134312} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134337} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272123} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272125} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326873} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326899} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351263} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354525} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366125} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366379} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366525} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414752}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Orthogonal Basis For the Column Space of a Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "09154627-fce0-54d9-b9c1-232fc06abaf2" :step/math-academy-id 20259 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134687}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134687 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135055 :tutorial/content "Given a basis $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2\\}$ for a subspace $V,$ we can use the Gram-Schmidt process to find an *orthonormal* basis of the subspace.\n\nTo do this, we simply execute the Gram-Schmidt process as before to obtain an orthogonal basis $\\{\\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2\\},$ and then we normalize each vector in this basis to get the orthonormal basis $\\{\\mathbf{u}_1, \\mathbf{u}_2\\},$ where\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}_1 = \\dfrac{\\mathbf{v}_1}{\\Vert \\mathbf{v}_1 \\Vert},\n\t\\quad\n\t\\mathbf{u}_2 = \\dfrac{\\mathbf{v}_2}{\\Vert \\mathbf{v}_2 \\Vert}.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "1ea8b19b-f7a8-5339-abd4-3069e18b2555" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3632 :tutorial/title "Using the Gram-Schmidt Process to Find an Orthonormal Basis"} :step/id #uuid "881045a6-4243-5fbd-8617-d6520ecad604" :step/math-academy-id 20260 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134688}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134688 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133795 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133604 :question/id #uuid "60dbc829-01fc-5283-8741-68af483d5313" :question/math-academy-id "e-5583"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ccdefb8e-1bb5-52e9-812d-d2a5bffff731" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134310} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134311} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272129} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272130} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326874} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351265} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354526} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354527} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366118} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366386} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414750} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481433}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Orthonormal Basis of a Vector Space"} :step/id #uuid "5c0057df-8020-54b5-b1ba-1011db749edc" :step/math-academy-id 20261} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134770 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135089 :tutorial/content "Sometimes it's helpful to \"clean up\" the result of a Gram-Schmidt process by scaling the resulting vectors.\n\nFor example, suppose that the set $\\mathcal B,$ given by\n\n![](images/79/79d1a4963f0e8a2e47dda0acf5c6031571574b1631d57c15d757f94b4f25468b.svg)\n\nis an orthogonal basis for $\\textrm{Span} \\{ \\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2 \\}.$\n\nSince the vector $\\mathbf{v}_2$ lies in $\\textrm{Span} \\{ \\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2 \\}$ and $\\mathbf{v}_1 \\perp \\mathbf{v}_2,$ the vector $3\\mathbf{v}_2$ also lies in $\\textrm{Span} \\{ \\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2 \\}$ and $\\mathbf{v}_1 \\perp 3\\mathbf{v}_2.$ Therefore, taking \n\n![](images/c6/c69d0c835aa55e7e835c81a4338d56bd3fda97ebb8a73ed370e4384b80d23093.svg)\n\ninstead of $\\mathbf{v}_2,$ we again obtain an orthogonal basis for $\\textrm{Span} \\{ \\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2 \\}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/fc/fcdac688252b50d74ed8c4bddba578f027f3bfec5a356ec3fe54839638c1a0ae.svg)\n\nBut this time, the second vector in the basis looks slightly nicer and is probably easier to work with." :tutorial/id #uuid "00f219fa-5ae3-5e1d-acd0-7b7df752a433" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4351 :tutorial/title "Scaling the Vectors After the Gram-Schmidt Process"} :step/id #uuid "8e33ff42-ffc9-57ae-941b-8a447aded2e4" :step/math-academy-id 26458 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134686}}] :activity/title "The Gram-Schmidt Process for Two Vectors" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186134006}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046673} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414757} :activity/id #uuid "b568452d-b15b-5a64-b20e-4ac3b74285e9" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050578 :topic/id #uuid "5558fd2c-f6e0-5362-bf8b-7d3500df79a0" :topic/math-academy-id 2127} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414754 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414752 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60d1356a-94ea-5209-96d5-9d9418453443" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414730} {:answer/id #uuid "7ea97cd7-7fc3-5a3d-bc31-abc4a8a14911" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414734} {:answer/id #uuid "7edf9628-a06c-547d-b79b-2b8c3b2e00b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414735} {:answer/id #uuid "a319ab1d-aad5-5d1b-8415-3377cf75bf86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414739} {:answer/id #uuid "d937650c-2bf8-5168-848d-23a4e1590bfc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414743}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414735} :answer-field/id #uuid "546f2176-ce65-54a2-a805-2b4a861a5018" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414747}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9ea317f2-12b0-537c-bfd8-c2c151d6c85b" :question/math-academy-id "q-86849" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}],\\,B={[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]}\n$$\n\nConsider the subspace $\\text{Col}(A)$ and an orthogonal basis $B$ of $\\text{Col}(A),$ where $A$ and $B$ are given above. Given that $B$ is derived from $\\text{Col}(A)$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $\\frac{b}{a}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{Col}(A)$ is the span of the columns of $A.$  \n\nFirst, let's denote\n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{(-3)\\cdot (-2)+2\\cdot 1+5\\cdot 2}{(-2)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B={{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}={[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}$ is an orthogonal basis for $\\text{Col}(A).$\n\nFinally, $a=1,$ $b=0,$ and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{0}{1}=0.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "22215345-cf56-5cf6-8ec7-0d0ddc7f81fb"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414755 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414750 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "72cc1442-df79-5623-bff4-42eedddafdb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{10}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414732} {:answer/id #uuid "77e7d035-74cb-5a21-8a35-1b0d4898e245" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414733} {:answer/id #uuid "9aa54ac6-f50b-5137-953c-f00902b1d420" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414738} {:answer/id #uuid "be937ae3-0668-5fe5-9548-61b224487acf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414741} {:answer/id #uuid "c38c74ea-a21b-5c0e-bb44-84641a158c8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414742}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414732} :answer-field/id #uuid "969beab5-314b-57a6-99da-182f476a0e29" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414749}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "0934e8ac-174d-5792-a7fc-4ab5bbca2aae" :question/math-academy-id "q-41971" :question/problem "$$\nV=\\text{Span}{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]},\\,B={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{7} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{14} \\\\ \\frac{3\\sqrt{14}}{14}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]}\n$$\n\nConsider the subspace $V$ and an **orthonormal** basis $B$ of $V,$ given above. Given that $B$ is derived from $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $|a|+|b|+|c|?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's denote\n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{2\\cdot 2+4\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 3}{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{3}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we normalize each vector to get an orthonormal basis.\n\n- ${u}_{1}=\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{3}^{2}}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{7} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{14} \\\\ \\frac{3\\sqrt{14}}{14}\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- ${u}_{2}=\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{3}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ \\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{10} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10}\\end{aligned}].$\n\nTherefore, $B={{u}_{1},{u}_{2}}={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{7} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{14} \\\\ \\frac{3\\sqrt{14}}{14}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ \\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{10} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10}\\end{aligned}]}$ is an orthonormal basis for $V.$\n\nFinally, $a=0,$ $b=\\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{10},$ $c=-\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10}$ and  \n\n$$\n|a|+|b|+|c|=|\\,0\\,|+|\\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{10}|+|-\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10}|=\\frac{2\\sqrt{10}}{5}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "74c122ae-6f5f-55d9-980e-db9851e51f87" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414754}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414756 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134311 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "012fe31e-f759-58b8-9e32-2deea745562c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414720} {:answer/id #uuid "5a5c8c62-d50c-5ae9-8f35-24b24fc3d1ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414728} {:answer/id #uuid "632c6ed4-e8c2-5396-8c74-4461b4fdfa46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414731} {:answer/id #uuid "862d3a52-49ed-5b27-bb35-bc67945b8d32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414736} {:answer/id #uuid "94c4a1bf-b64c-5ddd-8ca9-6c65c5b9727e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414737}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414731} :answer-field/id #uuid "4a66add1-170c-5173-8823-07f893271a27" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414746}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "1ad7f9ba-9786-5d27-9e6a-080a7315cce4" :question/math-academy-id "q-40740" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}],\\,B={{u}_{1},{u}_{2}}={[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]}\n$$\n\nConsider the subspace $\\text{Col}(A)$ and an **orthonormal** basis $B$ of $\\text{Col}(A),$ where $A$ and $B$ are given above. Given that $B$ is derived from $\\text{Col}(A)$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $|a|+|b|+|c|?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{Col}(A)$ is the span of the columns of $A.$  \n\nFirst, let's denote\n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{3\\cdot (-1)+0\\cdot (-2)+(-3)\\cdot 2}{{1}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{2}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we normalize each vector to get an orthonormal basis:\n\n- ${u}_{1}=\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{2}^{2}}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- ${u}_{2}=\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+(-1)^{2}}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}].$\n\nTherefore, $B={{u}_{1},{u}_{2}}={[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}]}$ is an orthonormal basis for $\\text{Col}(A).$\n\nFinally, $a=\\frac{2}{3},$ $b=-\\frac{2}{3},$ $c=-\\frac{1}{3}$ and  \n\n$$\n|a|+|b|+|c|=|\\frac{2}{3}|+|-\\frac{2}{3}|+|-\\frac{1}{3}|=\\frac{5}{3}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "84989845-fb61-500c-b47d-0a19bc3d185f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414755}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414757 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414753 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "048ef5c8-0a10-53a5-bbcf-1d721a524144" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414721} {:answer/id #uuid "05da9795-05f5-5040-9da5-00cee056fe08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414722} {:answer/id #uuid "1326ecd9-681d-55f9-841c-c7013851f621" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414723} {:answer/id #uuid "292aa1fd-4f1b-5b3f-b502-e340aa086aa9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{12}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414726} {:answer/id #uuid "502fbad5-6108-585e-9a6e-c3c7e917352d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414727}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414723} :answer-field/id #uuid "219cb7dd-0360-5f51-af53-0c31b7c77c47" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414745}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c7bb7823-c38b-5250-a2f7-3b306162dae1" :question/math-academy-id "q-40736" :question/problem "$$\nV=\\text{Span}{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]},\\,B={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]}\n$$\n\nConsider the subspace $V$ and an orthogonal basis $B$ of $V,$ given above. Given that $B$ is derived from $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's denote \n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{3\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 1+2\\cdot (-2)}{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-2)^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{17}{6} \\\\ \\frac{11}{6} \\\\ \\frac{7}{3}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B={{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{17}{6} \\\\ \\frac{11}{6} \\\\ \\frac{7}{3}\\end{aligned}]}$ is an orthogonal basis for $V.$\n\nFinally, we have $a=\\frac{17}{6},$ $b=\\frac{11}{6},$ and \n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{(\\frac{17}{6})}{(\\frac{11}{6})}=\\frac{17}{11}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "99574453-db24-5adc-881e-60bd9dee9204" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414758}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414758 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414751 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18fa685c-26d9-50c3-9545-5630e0f95fcd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414724} {:answer/id #uuid "194427ad-263d-5d8c-83c2-9e6a530a1d0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414725} {:answer/id #uuid "5c1253b4-be89-5b75-90d7-9bf16f52d17f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414729} {:answer/id #uuid "a926e36b-6239-5512-942a-761db7b00ee5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414740} {:answer/id #uuid "f308a53e-6685-5d66-b2f6-d1b95a0af889" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414744}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414729} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ce877fc-f404-5538-a461-7601462b178c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414748}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "44c51145-6075-534b-b54c-46371542f3eb" :question/math-academy-id "q-40737" :question/problem "$$\nV=\\text{Span}\\{[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]\\}\n$$\n\nConsider the subspace $V$ and an orthogonal basis $B$ of $V,$ given above. Given that $B$ is derived from $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $\\frac{b}{a}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's denote \n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{4\\cdot 3+2\\cdot 0+(-3)\\cdot (-1)}{{3}^{2}+{0}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{3}{2}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 2 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}=\\{[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 2 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is an orthogonal basis for $V.$\n\nFinally, we have $a=-\\frac{1}{2},$ $b=2,$ and \n\n$$\n\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{2}{(-\\frac{1}{2})}=-4.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "a30ac731-6001-5775-b224-b7080f289ca2" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414756}}] :activity/title "The Gram-Schmidt Process for Two Vectors" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414719}]}
